its own VLAN\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"bridge vlan show confirms eth1 has vlan-id 50, PVID, Egress Untagged, both immediately after configuration (cmd-86e53da478664ada) and after node1's own restart (cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76), demonstrating persistence.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-86e53da478664ada\",\"cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76\",\"cmd-18eda3da25224377\",\"cmd-64911d96462d403f\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"node1's link to node3 (eth2) configured as an untagged access port in its own VLAN, different from node2's\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"bridge vlan show confirms eth2 has vlan-id 60, PVID, Egress Untagged, separate from eth1's VLAN 50, both at setup and after node1's restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-86e53da478664ada\",\"cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76\",\"cmd-18eda3da25224377\",\"cmd-64911d96462d403f\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"node2 keeps a working link to node1 inside its own VLAN\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node2 (192.168.50.2/24 on enp8s0, persisted via nmcli connection profile) successfully pings node1's SVI 192.168.50.1 before and after both node2's and node1's restarts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0632a04fc5f64b6f\",\"cmd-c34fc21a3de8466d\",\"cmd-d74b0cb463de4216\",\"cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76\"]},{\"id\":\"R4\",\"requirement\":\"node3 keeps a working link to node1 inside its own VLAN\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node3 (192.168.60.3/24 on enp8s0, persisted via nmcli connection profile) successfully pings node1's SVI 192.168.60.1 before and after both node3's and node1's restarts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ca6df100a1e64367\",\"cmd-c34fc21a3de8466d\",\"cmd-15465e36c5b14ab7\",\"cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76\"]},{\"id\":\"R5\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 stay separated from one another (mutual isolation across VLANs)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node2 cannot reach node3 by default ping, and even fails when an explicit static route via node1 is added (invalid gateway / 100% loss). bridge fdb on node1 shows eth1 only learns VLAN-50 MACs and eth2 only VLAN-60 MACs, confirming no cross-VLAN bridging. After node1's restart, isolation persists and is now explicitly attributed to disabled IPv4 forwarding, shown by a 'Destination Net Unreachable' ICMP response.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cccd3560bd3b4350\",\"cmd-d5cc8b1b05bf4440\",\"cmd-665d9028b0e94d5d\",\"cmd-2a12a8ad777c482e\",\"cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.88,\"summary\":\"Command trace shows disciplined, evidence-linked work. Early temporary IP assignments on eth1/eth2 (cmd-f8afe47967474cd5) and on node2/node3 enp8s0 (cmd-aea2bcb23db04a23, cmd-f98e331bf02b432d) were used only for link discovery and were removed shortly after (cmd-79bb418056c34833; node2/node3 addresses were already gone due to NetworkManager reverting non-profile changes, confirmed by cmd-97ba9ace11d74bf2/cmd-1a2468283cb14a89). A temporary static route added on node2 to more rigorously test isolation (cmd-cccd3560bd3b4350) was fully removed afterward (cmd-f33c45591fab4254) and directly supports the isolation requirement, so it is not an unnecessary mutation. Stopping and later restarting interfaces-config.service/networking.service on node1 (cmd-f4d00b9c5ee846df, cmd-6c0fc1e53ca8438b) was a necessary, bounded prerequisite for building the bridge and was restored. One genuine gap was caught and corrected: after node1's reboot, net.ipv4.conf.all.forwarding reverted to 1 because the sysctl.d file did not reliably apply to interfaces the oneshot unit created after boot (cmd-6add4e3c08dd4124, cmd-64911d96462d403f); the executor added an explicit sysctl -w call inside the persisted setup script (cmd-e47d55c225b84107) and re-verified forwarding=0, but this fix was validated only by restarting the service, not by a further full reboot cycle. Minor residue: a failed tcpdump attempt on node2 (cmd-92c74ce379f747c2, 'tcpdump: command not found') left an empty /tmp/t5.log file that persisted into the after-executor snapshot; this is trivial and did not affect functionality. Other diagnostic tcpdump logs on node1 were cleared by node1's own reboot (tmpfs).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f8afe47967474cd5\",\"cmd-79bb418056c34833\",\"cmd-aea2bcb23db04a23\",\"cmd-f98e331bf02b432d\",\"cmd-97ba9ace11d74bf2\",\"cmd-1a2468283cb14a89\",\"cmd-cccd3560bd3b4350\",\"cmd-f33c45591fab4254\",\"cmd-f4d00b9c5ee846df\",\"cmd-6c0fc1e53ca8438b\",\"cmd-6add4e3c08dd4124\",\"cmd-64911d96462d403f\",\"cmd-e47d55c225b84107\",\"cmd-92c74ce379f747c2\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct, before/after-restart evidence: node1's eth1 and eth2 are configured as untagged access ports in distinct VLANs (50 and 60) via a vlan-filtering bridge with per-VLAN SVIs, node2 and node3 each retain working connectivity to node1 within their own VLAN, and the two hosts remain mutually isolated (confirmed both by default behavior and by a deliberate attempt to route around the isolation, plus FDB evidence of no cross-VLAN MAC learning). The provider-wide restart protocol was executed for all three nodes with distinct pre/post boot IDs and recovered state, though on node1 a real reboot exposed that the IPv4-forwarding-disable sysctl did not survive reboot as originally configured; the executor diagnosed and fixed this by embedding the sysctl call in the persisted startup script, but the fix itself was only validated by a service restart rather than a second full reboot.\",\"limitations\":[\"The corrected forwarding-disable persistence fix on node1 (cmd-e47d55c225b84107) was validated only via 'systemctl restart vlan-access.service', not via an additional full reboot, so full confidence in its reboot-durability is not directly demonstrated.\",\"Executor's own final report claims 'proving changed boot IDs and full post-restart recovery' but the raw timeline shows an intermediate failed recovery state on node1 (forwarding=1, vlan-access.service initially inactive) that was only resolved after manual intervention; this nuance is understated in the executor's summary.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"node1's link to node2 (eth1) configured as an untagged access port in its own VLAN","status":"satisfied","summary":"bridge vlan show confirms eth1 has vlan-id 50, PVID, Egress Untagged, both immediately after configuration (cmd-86e53da478664ada) and after node1's own restart (cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76), demonstrating persistence.","evidence_ids":["cmd-86e53da478664ada","cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76","cmd-18eda3da25224377","cmd-64911d96462d403f"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"node1's link to node3 (eth2) configured as an untagged access port in its own VLAN, different from node2's","status":"satisfied","summary":"bridge vlan show confirms eth2 has vlan-id 60, PVID, Egress Untagged, separate from eth1's VLAN 50, both at setup and after node1's restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-86e53da478664ada","cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76","cmd-18eda3da25224377","cmd-64911d96462d403f"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"node2 keeps a working link to node1 inside its own VLAN","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 (192.168.50.2/24 on enp8s0, persisted via nmcli connection profile) successfully pings node1's SVI 192.168.50.1 before and after both node2's and node1's restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0632a04fc5f64b6f","cmd-c34fc21a3de8466d","cmd-d74b0cb463de4216","cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"node3 keeps a working link to node1 inside its own VLAN","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3 (192.168.60.3/24 on enp8s0, persisted via nmcli connection profile) successfully pings node1's SVI 192.168.60.1 before and after both node3's and node1's restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ca6df100a1e64367","cmd-c34fc21a3de8466d","cmd-15465e36c5b14ab7","cmd-df112d5b6f4f4d76"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"node2 and node3 stay separated from one another (mutual isolation across VLANs)","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 cannot reach 